# THE IDOLM@STER CINDERELLA GIRLS — Episode 11
## Retrospective Music and Sound Audit
### From incompatible drafts to audible ratification: Asterisk becomes a unit by sounding different together

## Scope

The canonical Episode-11 reading already established the central relational conclusion:

> Miku Maekawa and Riina Tada do not become compatible by discovering that they were secretly the same. Their unit becomes viable when persistent difference can be treated as `持ち味` and each member can protect the other's `拘り`.

The v2 sound audit substantially strengthens that reading because Episode 11 does not merely **tell** us that the two have learned to respect their differences. It makes the process audible at three different levels:

1. **creative labor** — the girls voice and reject incompatible lyric ideas before producing a shared song;
2. **live social coordination** — Miku's cat-idol audience call becomes something Riina can enter without becoming a copy of Miku;
3. **formal episode structure** — their completed performance is heard before the quiet office scene in which they explicitly ratify the pairing and only afterward acquire the name Asterisk.

The governing sonic refinement is:

> **Episode 11 makes compatibility an audible achievement. Miku and Riina first fail to make their individual vocabularies coexist, then learn to enter one another's performance grammar, and finally produce a song capable of carrying both identities at once.**

This is stronger than a generic "opposites attract" reading. The episode is interested in **co-authorship under persistent difference**.

