The song's verbal field deliberately contains both sides of the earlier conflict.

Riina-coded material invokes:

- heat;
- COOL/rock language;
- forward drive.

Miku-coded material invokes:

- cuteness;
- ribbon;
- tail/cat imagery.

The song then places those distinctions inside shared plural and forward-moving language.

The retrospective significance is not that every lyric can be assigned mechanically to one girl.

The stronger and safer conclusion is formal:

> **the final musical object has enough room to contain signs that were previously treated as mutually obstructive.**

The pair does not solve difference by reducing the song to a neutral midpoint stripped of both identities.

Nor does one identity simply defeat the other.

The song turns their disagreement into **arrangement material**.

That is the sonic counterpart to the later line that they can continue while respecting one another's `拘り`.

